  "transcript": "# Consumer protection\n\n## Most Black Friday ‘deals’ aren’t unique bargains\n> Which? found the majority of advertised Black Friday offers could be found at the same or lower prices at other times.\n\nChair: Three big firms—Amazon, John Lewis and TikTok—refused to attend today’s hearing, which is disappointing given the complaints about Black Friday marketing.\nMr Reynolds: Sue, I’ve seen examples: a John Lewis Samsung washing machine listed at a Black Friday price that was on sale 61% of the year, an Amazon toaster that was cheaper two months before Black Friday, and a TV with an RRP never actually used in the past year. What are the worst examples you’ve seen?\nSue Davies: Our monitoring last year of 175 Black Friday deals across eight retailers found 83% could be bought at the same or lower price at other times. Many advertised bargains are misleading rather than genuine special offers.\nChair: That’s 80% plus of deals not being true deals?\nSue Davies: Yes. We see fake reference pricing—‘was’ prices or RRPs presented as if they were the usual price when they’re not—and tactics like drip pricing and countdown clocks that pressure shoppers.\nMr Reynolds: If John Lewis or others are misleading customers, why do they keep doing it?\nSue Davies: Because, largely, they can get away with it. The law and enforcement aren’t strong enough to deter misleading pricing in many cases.\nMr Reynolds: You flagged Currys and Very as problems as well?\nSue Davies: Yes—those and Sports Direct repeatedly use reference prices to suggest larger discounts than are real.\n\n\n## Enforcement is stretched and legally awkward\n> Trading standards are under-resourced and current legal tests make it hard to act against misleading pricing.\n\nChair: Why is enforcement failing? Is it resources or the law?\nSue Davies: Both. Local trading standards have been cut hard—our FOI work showed in places one person can be responsible for hundreds of laws—so they lack capacity. Also legal hurdles exist for proving a price misled the 'average consumer' in a transactional decision.\nDavid MacKenzie: Local authorities use primary authority partnerships where one council advises a big company, which helps in some sectors. But enforcement teams are tiny compared with the multinationals they must police, creating a large imbalance.\nSue Davies: We need legal updates—making it easier to act on misleading reference pricing—and more resources so trading standards can investigate properly rather than being spread thin across product safety, rogue traders and other duties.\n\n\n## Social platforms, scam ads and AI make the problem worse\n> Scam advertising and AI-generated fakes on social platforms compound misleading pricing and need Ofcom and platform action.\n\nAntonia Bance: TikTok's retail ad revenue is surging. Are consumers more at risk of scams on social platforms during Black Friday, and where are those risks appearing?\nDavid MacKenzie: Yes. Alongside pricing tricks, scammers use AI to create fake reviews, fake influencer endorsements and misleading ads on marketplaces and social shops. Platforms should be using their own tech to stop this.\nSue Davies: Which? pressed for scam ads to be covered by the Online Safety Act. Ofcom now has powers, but it must enforce them. We still see scam advertising across social media and need stronger action and platform accountability.\nAntonia Bance: So platforms like TikTok Shop need to be more active in policing adverts and removing scams?\nDavid MacKenzie: Absolutely—these tech firms can and should deploy AI and moderation to reduce illicit listings, fake endorsements and scam ads on their services.\n\n\n## New laws help but enforcement and scope need strengthening\n> The DMCC/DMCC-style powers and CMA fines are a start, but more focused enforcement and legal clarity are required.\n\nDan Aldridge: Is the Digital Markets, Competition and Consumers Act and CMA work delivering consumer protection, or could it be faster and tougher?\nDavid MacKenzie: There’s a good start: the CMA has imposed around £12 million in fines and secured refunds in cases. But it’s only the start—cases take time, trading standards still must do much local work, and we need to target the biggest harms.\nDan Aldridge: So it helps, but it needs to be 'turbocharged'—more resources and sharper enforcement priorities?\nDavid MacKenzie: Yes. We must prioritise areas of highest consumer detriment and make sure regulators and local teams have the powers and funding to act quickly.\nMr Reynolds: Are there quick legal fixes—for example, banning the use of RRPs?\nDavid MacKenzie: That’s one idea. If RRPs serve no legitimate purpose, removing or strictly limiting them would stop a common route to fake discounts.\n\n\n## Payment protections and a short note on fireworks\n> New payment methods like pay-by-bank leave protection gaps; fireworks enforcement is mostly effective but antisocial use is the bigger problem.\n\nMr Reynolds: On pay-by-bank (open banking direct transfers): should consumers be worried because they lose card protections like section 75 and chargeback?\nSue Davies: Yes—we’re concerned. One in eight people don’t realise pay-by-bank lacks card protections. If a seller goes bust or goods are faulty, consumers could lose large sums. We need a baseline level of protection for these payments.\nDavid MacKenzie: There are bank refund rules for some frauds, but not for common problems like supplier insolvency or contract breaches. The differences between debit/credit protections and pay-by-bank need clarifying and possibly expanding.\nMr Reynolds: Does section 75 still make sense given new payment routes and intermediaries like PayPal complicating coverage?\nDavid MacKenzie: Section 75 hasn’t fully kept pace. Adding intermediaries can remove protections. We need clarity from the Financial Ombudsman and possible updates to reflect modern payments.\nChair: A final quick point on fireworks: would tighter rules drive an illicit market?\nDavid MacKenzie: Bans can increase black markets—scale is hard to predict. Scotland restricted sales and the number of legitimate retailers fell, but we haven’t seen evidence of a huge illicit supply increase. The main issue remains antisocial use rather than legal retail supply.\nChair: Thanks—these points will go to Ministers as we press for legal tightening, clearer payment protections and better-funded enforcement.\n",
